# Allowlist for scripts/scan-secret-values.sh — EXACT literal values only, each with a reason.
#
# WHY AN ALLOWLIST AND NOT A SMARTER REGEX. The scanner correctly flagged `decisions.md`, because the
# defect records quote the laundered inputs verbatim so the next reader can see what got through.
# The tempting fixes — skip fenced code blocks, skip inline-backticked text — would carve exactly the
# hole a real pasted credential hides in. An explicit list of literals cannot silently broaden, and
# every entry is reviewable. Same reasoning as `.gitleaksignore`.
#
# THIS FILE IS A BYPASS SURFACE. It is CODEOWNER-protected. Adding a line is a security decision.
# Never add a value that is or ever was live. Never add a pattern — literals only.

AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE   # the AWS-published documentation key. Quoted in decisions.md (GDF-010) and gdf-check.sh as the exact input a trailing comment laundered. The ONLY provider-shaped literal in this repo, and it is the one string every scanner already knows is a doc sample.
F1XTURE0DEMO0VALUE0NOTREAL1234         # synthetic; the GDF-013 comment-laundering demo, quoted in decisions.md
changeme_F1XTURE0DEMO0NOTREAL0VALUE    # synthetic; the placeholder-prefix laundering demo, quoted in decisions.md

# ── NEVER add a conformance fixture value to this list. ───────────────────────────────────────
# The fixtures exist to prove the detector fires; allowlisting one silently disarms the harness that
# guards the detector. This exact collision happened while writing this file: `AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE`
# was allowlisted for the decisions.md record and immediately turned `laundered-by-comment.yaml`
# green. The self-test caught it in one run. Fixtures carry `F1XTURE`/`f1xture` markers instead.
#
# ── AND NEVER GIVE A FIXTURE A PROVIDER SIGNATURE. ────────────────────────────────────────────
# GDF-014. The first version of these fixtures used `xoxb-…` and `whsec_…` and `AKIA…` because
# realistic-looking values felt like better tests. GitHub push protection rejected the entire v1.2
# push over two of them. It was right, and the reason matters more than the inconvenience: a fixture
# shaped like a live credential trips every downstream scanner, and the only way to ship it is to
# teach the operator to click "allow this secret." That habit is what leaks the real ones.
# A fixture must be long, high-entropy and UNMISTAKABLY FAKE. Detection depends on key name, length
# and shape — never on a provider prefix — so nothing is lost.
